      "message": "**File:** `util/focaltech/usb_context.h` (Line \\~135)\n\n**Corrected Snippet:**\n\n```c\n  // Wait for device removal (hotplug event)\n  int ResetComplete(int timeout_sec) const;\n  void UsbResetRecvPipe() const; // Removed (void)\n\n```\n\n* **Rationale:** In standard C, an empty parameter list `()` means a function can take an unspecified number of arguments, so `(void)` was used to strictly define zero arguments. In C++, `()` strictly means zero arguments. Using `(void)` is an outdated C idiom that adds unnecessary visual clutter in modern C++.  \n* **Google Style Guide:** While implicit in the overall mandate to use modern C++ standard features over legacy C paradigms, this is universally accepted as standard C++ practice. (See [C++ Core Guidelines NL.25](https://isocpp.github.io/CppCoreGuidelines/CppCoreGuidelines#rl-void)).",
